WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.850
Grueling auditions, fierce competition from
the world's biggest stars, and an endorsement

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.600
from Denzel Washington.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.360
These facts about Baz Luhrmann's "Elvis" will
make your jaw drop.

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:16.750
Austin Butler hadn't given much thought to
playing one of the world's most iconic figures

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.539
in music before 2018.

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:22.570
However, an Elvis Christmas song sparked something
in him, as he told Deadline,

00:00:35.924 --> 00:00:40.910
That friend was actually his then-girlfriend
Vanessa Hudgens, and she even suggested that

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.140
Butler should try to get the rights to write
an Elvis biopic.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.830
According to Butler, Hudgens brought up the
idea again a few weeks later while he was

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.360
playing the piano, but he dismissed the suggestion.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.780
A week later, his agent told him about Baz
Luhrmann's movie, and Butler recalled to Deadline,

00:01:03.728 --> 00:01:09.110
Austin Butler underwent a long audition process
for "Elvis," which mostly consisted of him

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.850
reading the script and singing for Baz Luhrmann
over and over again.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.630
As he admitted on "The Kelly Clarkson Show,"

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:16.610
"We ended up doing that for five months.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.327
It was unlike any audition."

00:01:19.327 --> 00:01:24.530
Before that grueling process began, Butler
sent the director a self-tape of himself playing

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:26.979
the piano and singing "Unchained Melody."

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:31.720
Prior to filming the tape, Butler discovered
that Elvis' mom died when the superstar was

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.950
just 23-years-old.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:39.909
Butler was the same age when he lost his mom,
too, which of course influenced his performance.

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:42.180
The former Disney Channel star told Deadline,

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:02.220
The video was raw and emotional, and Luhrmann
told Variety that he was blown away by Butler's performance.

00:02:02.605 --> 00:02:07.000
Austin Butler faced some fierce competition
for the role of Elvis.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.590
Before he was officially in the running, Baz
Luhrmann screen-tested many other actors and

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.700
singers, including Harry Styles.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.540
In fact, Styles was reportedly one of the
frontrunners for the role before Butler entered

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:19.540
the ring.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.390
During an interview on Howard Stern’s SiriusXM
show, Styles explained,

00:02:32.992 --> 00:02:37.143
Unfortunately, there was one major problem
with casting the former One Direction singer.

00:02:37.143 --> 00:02:38.961
Director Luhrmann explained to People,

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:47.430
Meanwhile, the casting agent for "Elvis" told
Variety that there were other names in the

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.430
mix, too.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:52.569
Firstly, Miles Teller, who showed off his
musical chops by singing the rock and roll

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:56.800
hit "Great Balls of Fire" in "Top Gun: Maverick,"
was up for the part of Elvis.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.120
Additionally, "West Side Story" star Ansel
Elgort was reportedly on Luhrmann's radar

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:01.813
for the part.

00:03:01.813 --> 00:03:04.180
Ultimately, though, Butler beat them all.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.810
"Well I just felt so privileged to get to
go on that journey with Baz, it was just the

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.760
time of my life, I felt so happy."

00:03:10.858 --> 00:03:15.320
In an interview with Deadline, Austin Butler
revealed that from the moment he heard about

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.870
the movie, he started prepping for his five-month-long
audition process as though he'd already gotten

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:21.870
the role.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.269
Acting like he was already making the movie
was clearly a tactic that worked for the actor.

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:30.299
It also helped that Denzel Washington put
in a good word for the young actor, with whom

00:03:30.299 --> 00:03:34.700
he'd worked on a 2018 Broadway production
of Eugene O'Neill's "The Iceman Cometh."

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.159
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,
Luhrmann recalled,

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.260
"And he says, you're about to meet an actor
I've been on stage with."

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.599
It seems that Butler lived up to Washington's
recommendation, as he did nothing but live

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:03.510
as Elvis for the two years it took to film
the biopic.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.530
During a press conference at the Cannes Film
Festival, Butler admitted,

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.269
"Elvis" began pre-production in 2019.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:24.970
After casting the film's stars, preparations
were being made to begin filming in Australia

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.560
in March 2020.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:31.919
However, everything came to a grinding halt
when star Tom Hanks and his wife Rita Wilson

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:33.940
tested positive for COVID-19.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.789
Thankfully, the couple's symptoms weren't
too severe, and they were able to return to

00:04:37.789 --> 00:04:39.590
the United States two weeks later.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:43.360
Like most people, Luhrmann didn't anticipate
the longevity of the pandemic.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.520
While Hanks and Wilson were in quarantine,
and any cast and crew members who'd been in

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.096
contact with them were isolating, he tweeted,

00:04:55.145 --> 00:04:59.830
While no other members of the cast or crew
became infected with COVID-19, it was several

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:03.320
months before everyone was able to return
to set.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.550
Filming officially began on September 23,
2020.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:08.810
At the time, Luhrmann said in a statement
to Deadline,

00:05:26.063 --> 00:05:30.000
Butler later confirmed to GQ that filming
finished in March 2021.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:35.380
Despite telling director Baz Luhrmann that
he didn't really sing during one of his auditions,

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:39.870
Austin Butler ended up performing some of
the King's iconic songs in "Elvis."

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:44.100
According to Deadline, Luhrmann planned to
have a voice impersonator take over vocals

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.313
for Elvis' younger years because he wasn't
able to use the original recordings for copyright reasons.

00:05:49.313 --> 00:05:53.685
However, he was completely moved by Butler's
abilities, telling Deadline,

00:05:57.814 --> 00:05:59.258
It’s spine-tingling.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:02.740
We just started riffing, and hours would go
by."

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:07.840
As the movie progressed, Luhrmann decided
to blend Butler's voice with the King's original

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.620
music to show how his voice changed over the
years.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.410
Composer Elliot Wheeler told Collider,

00:06:23.072 --> 00:06:27.702
"There's people out there that say ever since
I played Elvis, my voice has changed."

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:34.930
Despite its upbeat musical numbers, "Elvis"
certainly couldn't be called a light-hearted feature.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:39.043
The film makes it clear from the start that
Elvis Presley didn't have it easy growing up.

00:06:39.043 --> 00:06:43.540
For instance, Elvis had a big hole in his
heart that came from losing his twin brother

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.294
at birth.

00:06:44.294 --> 00:06:48.530
He also dealt with the shame of his father
going to jail, and from being dirt poor for

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:50.090
a long period of time.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:51.800
As director Baz Luhrmann told Deadline,

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:06.440
Luhrmann spent a lot of time researching Elvis
as a person to make his movie more authentic,

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.600
even if it was uncomfortable.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.270
As the director told Deadline,

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:27.470
As well as portraying the real Elvis, Baz
Luhrmann wanted the movie to be a political commentary.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:29.490
Speaking to GQ about the film, he explained,

00:07:42.109 --> 00:07:47.430
The film contextualizes Elvis' role in so
many historical moments, such as his reaction

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.290
to Martin Luther King Jr.'s death.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:53.870
Additionally, Tom Hanks' character, Colonel
Tom Parker, is an obvious commentary on greed

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:56.169
and corruption, with Luhrmann telling Deadline,

00:08:06.424 --> 00:08:09.520
Becoming Elvis Presley is no easy task.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.990
Along with studying the King's music and speech
patterns, Austin Butler had to put in the

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:17.690
work to physically transform into the rock
and roll legend.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.510
Butler worked with a strength trainer who
was instrumental in teaching him how to move

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.370
his body properly for the role.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.960
This training went on for months, but once
it came time for filming, Butler had to spend

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.650
his days in costume.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:34.810
This meant getting Elvis' makeup and hair
exactly right, a transformation alone that

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:38.589
took approximately four and a half hours to
complete.

00:08:38.589 --> 00:08:43.440
In addition to dying his hair, Butler went
through six wigs while filming the feature.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.959
In addition, the actor needed different prosthetics
to play the music legend at different ages.

00:08:47.959 --> 00:08:50.550
As the film's hair and makeup designer told
PopSugar,

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:08.420
Baz Luhrmann knew that Austin Butler was the
perfect choice to play Elvis.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:13.300
However, Elvis' real-life ex-wife Priscilla
Presley took a bit more convincing.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:14.810
As Luhrmann admitted to Deadline,

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.000
However, after watching the film at the Cannes
Film Festival, Priscilla was completely moved.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.660
She praised Butler's performance in a private
letter to Luhrmann, which he shared with Deadline,

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:33.660
saying,

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:52.360
In "Elvis," Baz Luhrmann weaves a tangled
web of relationships, but in December 2022,

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:53.360
he told Deadline,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.160
Of course, the depth of this addictive love
is wholly apparent to viewers of the film,

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.280
and to Tom Hanks' character, who narrates
"Elvis."

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:17.089
In one scene, Colonel Parker even points out
how Elvis' love for Priscilla is nothing compared

00:10:17.089 --> 00:10:19.339
to the love he feels from the audience.

00:10:19.339 --> 00:10:25.579
Thus, the movie ends with Colonel Parker claiming
that's ultimately what killed the superstar.

00:10:25.579 --> 00:10:30.110
Austin Butler dedicated years of his life
to learning everything he could about the

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:31.660
King of Rock and Roll.

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:36.230
That period of time was intense, both mentally
and physically, so it's not surprising that

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:37.540
it changed him.

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:39.374
Speaking to Deadline, Butler revealed,

00:10:51.051 --> 00:10:55.080
Playing the King has also had an impact on
Butler's career.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.480
As the New York Post wrote in a review of
the movie,

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:11.470
Since "Elvis" came out, Butler has already
proven that his star is most definitely on

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:12.470
the rise.

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:16.779
He has two feature films coming out in 2023
including "Dune: Part 2."

00:11:16.779 --> 00:11:22.089
Speaking to GQ, director Baz Luhrmann compared
Butler's continuing success to Leonardo DiCaprio's

00:11:22.089 --> 00:11:23.589
after "Romeo and Juliet."

00:11:23.589 --> 00:11:28.117
So, while Elvis may have left the building,
Butler is here to stay.

